    "https://www.presby.edu/about/offices-and-services/human-resources/employment/major-gift-officer/": {
      "title": "Major Gift Officer",
      "description": "Major Gift Officer | Presbyterian College Press Option+1 for screen-reader mode, Option+0 to cancel Accessibility Screen-Reader Guide, Feedback, and Issue Reporting | New window Skip to content Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Apply Info Visit Give Open\n                            Search Search for: Open Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Admissions Admissions Admissions Why PC First-Year Students Transfer Students International Students Admitted Students Graduate Students Visit Us Academics Academics Academics at PC Majors, Minors, & Special Programs Academic Resources Civil Conversations Heart of Service Center for Inquiry, Research & Scholarship Undergraduate Programs Graduate Programs Student Life Student Life Student Life Get Involved Health & Wellness Living on Campus Spiritual Life Career & Professional Development Blue Hose Athletics Cost & Aid Cost & Aid Cost & Aid Affordability Scholarships Grants Loans FAFSA Net Price Calculator Student Employment Tuition & Fees Veteran Benefits About PC About PC About Presbyterian College Leadership History, Mission & Plan Justice & Community Wellbeing For The Community Governance & Policies Accreditation Offices & Services News Giving Events FAQs Athletics Directory Alumni Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Major Gift Officer Major Gift Officer About PC Offices & Services Human Resources Employment Major Gift Officer Major Gift Officer This is an exciting time at Presbyterian College. As Presbyterian College approaches its sesquicentennial in 2030, the College is entering an exciting and transformational period of growth and momentum. The institution is preparing to launch the silent phase of a comprehensive campaign that will culminate in a public campaign celebration aligned with the College’s 150th anniversary. To support this ambitious vision, the Advancement team is expanding with the addition of a new Major Gift Officer position. This new role represents an extraordinary opportunity for relationship-centered fundraising professionals to help shape the future of Presbyterian College while joining a collaborative, mission-driven advancement team committed to innovation, stewardship, and meaningful donor engagement. At Presbyterian College, relationships matter. Our ethos is paramount with an emphasis on the liberal arts, and our direction as a church-related institution. Our advancement culture is collaborative, energetic, mission-focused, and deeply personal. We believe fundraising is about building authentic connections, telling meaningful stories, and helping people invest in lives transformed through a liberal arts education. This is an opportunity to: Join a growing and energized Advancement team. Build meaningful relationships with alumni and supporters who care deeply about PC. Play an important role in a historic comprehensive campaign. Partner with passionate colleagues across campus. Help shape the future of Presbyterian College. Candidates should bring strong communication and relationship-building skills, professional experience in fundraising, sales, higher education, nonprofit leadership, or related fields, and a genuine enthusiasm for connecting people with purpose and impact. Experience in major gift fundraising, campaigns, or higher education is welcomed but not required. If you are energized by working with people, inspired by mission-driven work, and excited to be part of a team building something meaningful together, we encourage you to apply. Please submit your resume and three professional references to hr@presby.edu . Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled, with priority given to those received by June 26. We invite you to review the job description below for a full overview of the position. About Presbyterian College Founded in 1880, Presbyterian College is a nationally recognized liberal arts college grounded in the ideals of service, faith, leadership, and academic excellence. Located in the historic town of Clinton, South Carolina, PC is a close-knit academic community where students are known personally, challenged intellectually, and prepared to lead lives of purpose and impact. PC is approximately 40 minutes from Greenville and Spartanburg, South Carolina and about one hour from Columbia, South Carolina. The region offers abundant opportunities for social and recreational activities, with easy access to Asheville and Charlotte, North Carolina, and Atlanta, Georgia. Presbyterian College is an equal opportunity employer committed to diversity and inclusion in all aspects of campus life. They encourage applications from candidates of all backgrounds who share their commitment to academic excellence, personal development, and service to others. The College provides equal employment opportunities without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or other legally protected characteristics. Position Description Department: Advancement Reports To: Assistant Vice President of Advancement Classification: Full-Time, Exempt Location: Clinton, South Carolina Position Summary Presbyterian College seeks a dynamic, energetic, and relationship-oriented Major Gift Officer to identify, cultivate, solicit, and steward philanthropic support from alumni, parents, friends, foundations, and community partners capable of making leadership-level gifts. The Major Gift Officer will manage a portfolio of prospective donors and play a central role in advancing institutional priorities during a pivotal period in the College’s history. Working closely with advancement colleagues, academic and administrative leaders, volunteers, and the President, the Major Gift Officer will develop meaningful partnerships that inspire philanthropic investment in Presbyterian College’s mission and strategic priorities. The successful candidate will thrive in a highly relational environment, demonstrate exceptional communication and interpersonal skills, and bring a genuine appreciation for the transformative impact of liberal arts education. The selected candidate will be mission-aligned with Presbyterian College’s impact as a church-related, nationally ranked liberal arts institution; engaging as a member of the community and seeking to make a difference in the lives of our students and the College. Essential Responsibilities Fundraising & Donor Engagement Manage and grow a portfolio of major gift prospects and donors capable of making gifts at the major gift levels. Develop and execute individualized cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship strategies that deepen engagement and increase philanthropic support. Conduct substantive donor visits and virtual meetings with alumni, parents, and friends of the College. Prepare and present compelling proposals and gift opportunities aligned with institutional priorities. Collaborate with advancement leadership, the Vice President, faculty, and volunteer leaders on strategic donor engagement. Advance campaign priorities through intentional prospect management and relationship development. Maintain timely and accurate donor records, contact reports, and strategy updates in the College’s CRM. Campaign Support Support the planning and execution of the College’s comprehensive campaign leading into the sesquicentennial celebration in 2030. Assist with campaign prospect identification, pipeline development, and leadership donor strategies. Partner with advancement colleagues to ensure coordinated and donor-centered campaign communications and experiences. Collaboration & Leadership Work collaboratively across the Advancement division, including alumni engagement, annual giving, stewardship, advancement services, and communications Serve as a positive ambassador for Presbyterian College both on and off campus. Participate in selected campus events, donor gatherings, and regional engagement activities. Contribute to a culture of teamwork, innovation, accountability, and mission-centered fundraising. Required Qualifications Bachelor’s degree required. Commitment to the mission and values of Presbyterian college, as a national, church related, liberal arts college Demonstrated success in fundraising, sales, business development, relationship management, higher education, nonprofit leadership, or a related field. Demonstrated success cultivating, soliciting, and closing gifts of $50,000 or more. Exceptional interpersonal, written, and verbal communication skills. Ability to build authentic relationships with diverse constituencies. Strong organizational skills and attention to detail. Ability to work independently while contributing positively to a collaborative team environment. Willingness and ability to travel regularly, including some evenings and weekends. Commitment to the mission and values of Presbyterian College. Preferred Qualifications Experience participating in campaign fundraising initiatives. Experience in higher education or nonprofit advancement environments. Familiarity with moves management principles and donor database systems. Appreciation for the value of a liberal arts education and student-centered community. Knowledge of or affinity for Presbyterian College and its mission . Learn More About PC About PC History, Mission, & Plan Benefits & Resources Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: 503 South Broad Street Clinton, South Carolina 29325 800.476.7272 Contact Us facebook instagram x flickr Quick Links & Resources Quick Links & Resources News Events Directory Athletics Campus Map Living on Campus Academic Calendar Course Catalog Offices & Facilities Accreditation Job Opportunities Justice & Community Wellbeing First-Gen Students International Students Accessible Education Health & Wellness Single-Sign On BannerWeb Brightspace Campus Bookstore Library Online Payment Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: © Copyright 2026 Presbyterian College | Title IX Policies Consumer Information Campus Police Presbyterian College is committed to providing an accessible and inclusive online experience. If you are unable to access any part of this website due to a disability, please contact Accessible Education at accommodations@presby.edu for assistance. NOTICE OF NONDISCRIMINATORY POLICY AS TO STUDENTS: Presbyterian College is an equal educational opportunity institution. The College’s admission standards and practices are free from discrimination on the basis of age, sex, race, creed, color, disability, ethnicity or national origin. Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community English Spanish en"
    },
    "https://www.presby.edu/about/offices-and-services/human-resources/employment/vice-president-of-enrollment-management/": {
      "title": "Vice President of Enrollment Management",
      "description": "Vice President of Enrollment Management | Presbyterian College Press Option+1 for screen-reader mode, Option+0 to cancel Accessibility Screen-Reader Guide, Feedback, and Issue Reporting | New window Skip to content Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Apply Info Visit Give Open\n                            Search Search for: Open Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Admissions Admissions Admissions Why PC First-Year Students Transfer Students International Students Admitted Students Graduate Students Visit Us Academics Academics Academics at PC Majors, Minors, & Special Programs Academic Resources Civil Conversations Heart of Service Center for Inquiry, Research & Scholarship Undergraduate Programs Graduate Programs Student Life Student Life Student Life Get Involved Health & Wellness Living on Campus Spiritual Life Career & Professional Development Blue Hose Athletics Cost & Aid Cost & Aid Cost & Aid Affordability Scholarships Grants Loans FAFSA Net Price Calculator Student Employment Tuition & Fees Veteran Benefits About PC About PC About Presbyterian College Leadership History, Mission & Plan Justice & Community Wellbeing For The Community Governance & Policies Accreditation Offices & Services News Giving Events FAQs Athletics Directory Alumni Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Vice President of Enrollment Management Vice President of Enrollment Management About PC Offices & Services Human Resources Employment Vice President of Enrollment Management Vice President of Enrollment Management Overview Presbyterian College seeks its next Vice President for Enrollment Management to serve as a strategic partner to the President and a member of the President’s Cabinet . This leader will guide comprehensive enrollment strategies that support sustainable growth, strong net tuition revenue, and academic quality in alignment with the College’s mission . Position The Vice President, Enrollment Management reports directly to the President and serves as a member of the President’s Cabinet. This senior executive position has strategic responsibility for leading all aspects of enrollment management at Presbyterian College, including undergraduate and graduate admissions, financial aid, recruitment, retention, and student yield strategies. The Vice President for Enrollment Management holds primary accountability for achieving enrollment-driven net tuition revenue targets and works in close partnership with the Vice President of Finance and Administration to align enrollment strategy, financial aid policy, and pricing decisions with institutional sustainability goals. The Vice President provides visionary leadership to develop and implement comprehensive enrollment strategies that ensure sustained positive enrollment growth, optimize net tuition revenue, and maintain high academic standards while supporting the College’s mission and strategic objectives. This role requires exceptional strategic thinking, collaborative leadership, commitment to mentoring and developing staff, and deep understanding of enrollment management best practices in the competitive higher education landscape. Position Description Strategic Enrollment Leadership and Planning Develop and implement comprehensive enrollment management strategies for both undergraduate and graduate programs that align with institutional strategic goals and market dynamics; Provide sound, consistent, and strategic direction for all enrollment processes, focusing on sustained positive enrollment and revenue results across all academic programs; Think creatively about enrollment strategy while maintaining and continuously improving high academic standards for admission at both undergraduate and graduate levels; Guide strategic discussions regarding multiple and often competing enrollment goals including class size, academic quality, net tuition revenue, and student diversity; Analyze national and international market dynamics and challenges to provide context and broad perspective for institutional planning. Serve as a key partner to the Vice President of Finance and Administration in aligning enrollment strategy with institutional financial planning, including tuition pricing, revenue projections, and long-term sustainability modeling. Support the President in communicating enrollment-related financial strategies and outcomes to the Board of Trustees, as appropriate. Team Leadership and Organizational Development Direct the undergraduate and graduate admissions and financial aid operations as an integrated, high-performing team; Recruit, develop, mentor, and inspire professional staff to achieve exceptional performance and career growth, fostering a culture of continuous learning and professional development; Champion the critical importance of personal interaction and relationship-building throughout the enrollment process, ensuring staff understand that a genuine human connection is central to successful recruitment and student decision-making; Manage and optimize student recruitment processes to meet entering class size, academic quality, and net tuition revenue objectives for all programs; Create and sustain a culture of shared ownership of enrollment management across the campus community; Educate the campus community about best practices in enrollment management and emerging trends in higher education. Financial Management and Revenue Optimization Manage net tuition revenue targets in close collaboration with the President’s Cabinet, especially the Vice President of Finance; Provide strategic leadership over institutional financial aid strategy, including discount rate management, aid leveraging, and scholarship optimization, ensuring alignment with enrollment goals, student success outcomes, and long-term financial sustainability. Use data-informed modeling to evaluate the enrollment and financial impact of financial aid policies and recommend adjustments as market conditions evolve. Develop and maintain enrollment and net tuition revenue forecasts, including scenario modeling and sensitivity analysis, and communicate risks and opportunities clearly to the President and Cabinet to support timely decision-making. Ensure strategic balance among entering class size, financial aid expenditures, tuition pricing, and net tuition revenue goals; Assess and evaluate the effectiveness of Presbyterian College’s financial aid policies and strategies; Determine optimal ways to leverage institutional and other resources to attract a talented, diverse, and engaged student body; Develop and implement merit-based scholarship and need-based financial aid strategies that maximize enrollment and revenue outcomes. Student Success and Retention Focus Recruit and enroll students who demonstrate high probability of success and graduation at Presbyterian College; Look beyond the entering class composition to focus on graduating class outcomes and long-term student success; Collaborate with Academic Affairs and Student Affairs to ensure seamless integration of enrollment and retention strategies; Implement data-driven approaches to identify and address factors affecting student persistence and completion; Develop innovative programs and initiatives that enhance the student experience from enrollment through graduation. Faculty and Campus Community Engagement Develop meaningful connections and communication channels with faculty members to ensure deep understanding of Presbyterian College’s academic programs and culture; Create shared understanding among faculty and staff of enrollment challenges, opportunities, and institutional priorities; Collaborate effectively with Academic Affairs to align enrollment strategies with curricular development and academic excellence initiatives; Foster cross-divisional partnerships that support comprehensive enrollment management objectives; Serve as a bridge between enrollment management and the broader academic mission of the College. Alumni and External Relations Coordinate with Alumni Relations to develop connections with alumni and deploy alumni resources as effective extensions of the admissions process; and utilize alumni energy and networks, particularly in securing student enrollment decisions and supporting recruitment efforts; Build and maintain relationships with secondary schools, guidance counselors, and educational partners; Represent Presbyterian College at professional conferences, educational forums, and industry events; Understand and respect Presbyterian College’s culture, character, values, and traditions while adapting to changing market conditions. Data Analysis and Continuous Improvement Utilize sophisticated data analytics and enrollment management software to inform strategic decision-making; Monitor and evaluate enrollment metrics, market trends, and competitive positioning on an ongoing basis; Provide regular reporting to the President and Cabinet on enrollment performance and strategic recommendations; Implement best practices in enrollment management while maintaining institutional distinctiveness; Lead assessment and evaluation efforts to ensure continuous improvement in enrollment outcomes. Qualifications Education and Experience Bachelor’s degree required; Master’s degree or advanced degree preferred; Minimum of seven years of substantial experience in comprehensive enrollment management in higher education, including oversight of both undergraduate and graduate admissions programs; Demonstrated record of regularly achieving enrollment and net revenue goals and improving institutional enrollment outcomes; Proven administrative experience with progressively responsible leadership roles in enrollment management; Experience in enrollment management at a private liberal arts institution with an understanding of the unique challenges and opportunities in this setting is preferred. Strategic Leadership and Management Exceptional record of strategic planning and implementation in enrollment management; Demonstrated emotional intelligence and ability to manage complex institutional priorities with creativity, clarity, and focus; Strong belief in the value of liberal arts education and commitment to Presbyterian College’s mission; Proven ability to embrace and advance diversity, equity, and inclusion initiatives. Professional Competencies Demonstrated expertise as a strategist in enrollment management with broad understanding of higher education trends; Strong motivation to achieve ambitious goals with track record of exceeding performance targets; Ability to establish clear directives and manage positive organizational change; Exceptional communication skills including ability to listen effectively and speak and write persuasively; Business acumen combined with deep understanding of the student experience in enrollment management. Personal Attributes and Leadership Qualities Ability to make difficult decisions while maintaining collaborative leadership style and welcoming personal demeanor; Grace under pressure with demonstrated resilience in challenging situations; Strong planning skills with attention to detail and ability to manage complex timelines; Commitment to the highest ethical standards in admission and financial aid practices; Credibility and strong networks among higher education professionals and organizations. Vision and Innovation Imaginative leader with vision and ability to see institutional possibilities and potential; Understanding of contemporary marketing principles, enrollment management software systems, and emerging technologies; Demonstrated success in building and maintaining effective enrollment management teams; Advanced analytical and critical thinking skills with ability to solve complex problems and develop creative solutions. Application Process ZRG Partners has been retained to conduct this search for Presbyterian College. Interested candidates should send a resume and cover letter as two separate PDF files to: Lori Hemmer Principal ZRG Partners lhemmer@zrgpartners.com Equal Opportunity Statement Presbyterian College is an equal opportunity employer committed to diversity and inclusion in all aspects of campus life. They encourage applications from candidates of all backgrounds who share their commitment to academic excellence, personal development, and service to others. The College provides equal employment opportunities without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or other legally protected characteristics. About Presbyterian College Inspired by the motto, “While We Live, We Serve,” Presbyterian College celebrates an enduring culture of academic rigor, honor, and service. PC is a nationally ranked liberal arts college with three graduate programs that provides students of all faiths, identities, and backgrounds with a transformative education to equip them for impactful careers and empower them to serve as powerful forces for positive change. PC’s distinctive approach uses innovative service as a tool of self-discovery, an amplifier of problem-solving skills, and a catalyst of curiosity which is epitomized by the newly developed Center for Inquiry, Research, and Scholarship (CIRAS). Located in Clinton, South Carolina, Presbyterian College combines academic excellence with a commitment to developing the whole person. Their close-knit community of approximately 1,200 students benefits from small class sizes, personalized attention, and a beautiful historic campus. As a college rooted in the Presbyterian tradition, PC values integrity, service, and the pursuit of knowledge in an environment that encourages both intellectual growth and character development. Presbyterian College is where students learn how to make a difference. It’s a place where they discover how to identify potential and opportunity in every challenge and develop the skills to take action. Here, students gain the foundation, knowledge, and community to improve the world around them and beyond. That’s the power of PC. Presbyterian College is committed to creating and maintaining a diverse, inclusive campus community that welcomes and supports students, faculty, and staff from all backgrounds. Learn More About PC About PC History, Mission, & Plan Benefits & Resources Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: 503 South Broad Street Clinton, South Carolina 29325 800.476.7272 Contact Us facebook instagram x flickr Quick Links & Resources Quick Links & Resources News Events Directory Athletics Campus Map Living on Campus Academic Calendar Course Catalog Offices & Facilities Accreditation Job Opportunities Justice & Community Wellbeing First-Gen Students International Students Accessible Education Health & Wellness Single-Sign On BannerWeb Brightspace Campus Bookstore Library Online Payment Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: © Copyright 2026 Presbyterian College | Title IX Policies Consumer Information Campus Police Presbyterian College is committed to providing an accessible and inclusive online experience. If you are unable to access any part of this website due to a disability, please contact Accessible Education at accommodations@presby.edu for assistance. NOTICE OF NONDISCRIMINATORY POLICY AS TO STUDENTS: Presbyterian College is an equal educational opportunity institution. The College’s admission standards and practices are free from discrimination on the basis of age, sex, race, creed, color, disability, ethnicity or national origin. Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community English Spanish en"
    },
    "https://www.presby.edu/about/offices-and-services/human-resources/employment/assistant-professor-of-chemistry/": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Chemistry | Presbyterian College Skip to content Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Apply Info Visit Give Open\r\n                            Search Search for: Open Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Admissions Admissions Why PC First-Year Students Transfer Students International Students Admitted Students Graduate Students Visit Us Academics Academics at PC Majors, Minors, & Special Programs Academic Resources Civil Conversations Heart of Service Center for Inquiry, Research & Scholarship Undergraduate Programs Graduate Programs Student Life Student Life Get Involved Health & Wellness Living on Campus Spiritual Life Career & Professional Development Blue Hose Athletics Cost & Aid Cost & Aid Affordability Scholarships Grants Loans FAFSA Net Price Calculator Student Employment Tuition & Fees Veteran Benefits About PC About Presbyterian College Leadership History, Mission & Plan Justice & Community Wellbeing For The Community Governance & Policies Accreditation Offices & Services News Giving Events FAQs Athletics Directory Alumni Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Assistant Professor of Chemistry Assistant Professor of Chemistry About PC Offices & Services Human Resources Employment Assistant Professor of Chemistry Assistant Professor of Chemistry Position Description The Presbyterian College Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the assistant professor level starting August 2027. This position is fully on-site with in-person lecture and laboratory instruction. Teaching expectations include Organic Chemistry with a combination of General Chemistry, and/or upper-level elective courses in the field of the candidate’s expertise. The successful candidates will also be expected to contribute to the undergraduate research program. The candidate will have the opportunity to be supported by the College’s NIH-funded South Carolina-INBRE research program, as an advisor in our PC Summer Fellows research program, and pursue other external funding. Minimum Requirements At a minimum, candidates should have a Ph.D. in Organic, Bioorganic, Medicinal, or related areas of chemistry. (ABD considered). To Apply Interested individuals should electronically send a single PDF containing a cover letter that describes your motivation for applying for this position; curriculum vitae ; statement of teaching philosophy; three letters of recommendation; and (official or unofficial) college and graduate transcripts to Provost Dr. Erin McAdams at [email protected] . For specific questions regarding the position, please contact Dr. A. Craig Powell, chair of the Chemistry Department, at [email protected] . Review of applications will begin October 1, 2026 , and continue until the position is filled. About Presbyterian College Presbyterian College (PC) is a liberal arts college affiliated with the Presbyterian Church USA. The college is committed to an equitable and inclusive learning environment for all students, supports academic freedom, and encourages community involvement. Located in Clinton, South Carolina, PC is approximately 40 minutes from Greenville and Spartanburg, and about one hour from Columbia. The region offers abundant opportunities for social and recreational activities, with easy access to Asheville and Charlotte, North Carolina, and Atlanta, Georgia. Presbyterian College is an EEO employer and does not discriminate against any person or group on the basis of age, color, disability, gender, pregnancy, national origin, race, religion, sexual orientation, veteran status, or genetic information. PC is building a culturally diverse faculty and staff committed to working in a multicultural environment and encourages applications from minorities and women. Learn More About PC About PC History, Mission, & Plan Benefits & Resources Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: 503 South Broad Street Clinton, South Carolina 29325 800.476.7272 Contact Us facebook instagram x flickr Quick Links & Resources Quick Links & Resources News Events Directory Athletics Campus Map Living on Campus Academic Calendar Course Catalog Offices & Facilities Accreditation Job Opportunities Justice & Community Wellbeing First-Gen Students International Students Accessible Education Health & Wellness Single-Sign On BannerWeb Brightspace Campus Bookstore Library Online Payment Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: © Copyright 2026 Presbyterian College | Title IX Policies Consumer Information Campus Police Presbyterian College is committed to providing an accessible and inclusive online experience. If you are unable to access any part of this website due to a disability, please contact Accessible Education at [email protected] for assistance. NOTICE OF NONDISCRIMINATORY POLICY AS TO STUDENTS: Presbyterian College is an equal educational opportunity institution. The College’s admission standards and practices are free from discrimination on the basis of age, sex, race, creed, color, disability, ethnicity or national origin. Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community"
    },
    "https://www.presby.edu/about/offices-and-services/human-resources/employment/assistant-professor-of-political-science/": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Political Science | Presbyterian College Skip to content Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Apply Info Visit Give Open\r\n                            Search Search for: Open Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Admissions Admissions Why PC First-Year Students Transfer Students International Students Admitted Students Graduate Students Visit Us Academics Academics at PC Majors, Minors, & Special Programs Academic Resources Civil Conversations Heart of Service Center for Inquiry, Research & Scholarship Undergraduate Programs Graduate Programs Student Life Student Life Get Involved Health & Wellness Living on Campus Spiritual Life Career & Professional Development Blue Hose Athletics Cost & Aid Cost & Aid Affordability Scholarships Grants Loans FAFSA Net Price Calculator Student Employment Tuition & Fees Veteran Benefits About PC About Presbyterian College Leadership History, Mission & Plan Justice & Community Wellbeing For The Community Governance & Policies Accreditation Offices & Services News Giving Events FAQs Athletics Directory Alumni Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Assistant Professor of Political Science Assistant Professor of Political Science About PC Offices & Services Human Resources Employment Assistant Professor of Political Science Assistant Professor of Political Science U.S. Politics / Pre-Law Studies (Tenure-Track) Position Description The Department of Political Science at Presbyterian College invites applications for a 9-month, tenure-track, assistant professor position in U.S. politics. (The Ph.D. in political science is strongly preferred, but ABD will be considered at the rank of instructor.) The successful candidate will also serve as Director of Pre-law Studies, with a start date of August 16, 2027. Review of applications will begin on October 15, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. The Department of Political Science is one of the largest on campus, as it typically graduates around 15-20 students each year. External observers highlight small class sizes, a well-developed curriculum, and a faculty committed to student learning and success. In that context, successful applicants should display a strong enthusiasm for teaching and a commitment to both service and scholarship. Responsibilities Responsibilities for this position include teaching in the Pre-Law minor, including two courses in constitutional law (governmental powers, and civil rights/liberties) as well as a course in legal studies. The ideal candidate will also teach an introductory course in U.S. government as well as upper-level courses in U.S. politics or public policy, with an open field of specialization. The teaching load is seven courses per academic year (4:3). In addition to teaching, responsibilities as Director of Pre-Law Studies include coordination of the College’s established pre-law program, advising Pre-Law minors and other students interested in law, and networking with law schools and other graduate programs, including opportunities in political science, public administration, or state and local politics. The ideal candidate will also contribute to student-focused activities, including the Pre-Law and Political Science clubs, South Carolina Student Legislature, and various on-campus events. To Apply Submission of application materials by electronic mail is strongly encouraged. Interested individuals should provide, at a minimum, a letter stating interest in the position, a curriculum vitae, transcripts from undergraduate and graduate institutions, and three letters of recommendation. Materials should be sent to the Office of the Provost at [email protected] . For individual questions, please contact Dr. David Liu, chair of the Department of Political Science, at [email protected] . About Presbyterian College Presbyterian College (PC) is a liberal arts college affiliated with the Presbyterian Church USA. The college is committed to an equitable and inclusive learning environment for all students, supports academic freedom, and encourages community involvement. Located in Clinton, South Carolina, PC is approximately 40 minutes from Greenville and Spartanburg, South Carolina and about one hour from Columbia, South Carolina. The region offers abundant opportunity for social and recreational activities, with easy access to Asheville and Charlotte, North Carolina, and Atlanta, Georgia. Presbyterian College is an EEO employer and does not discriminate against any person or group on the basis of age, color, disability, gender, pregnancy, national origin, race, religion, sexual orientation, veteran status or genetic information.  PC is building a culturally diverse faculty and staff committed to working in a multicultural environment and encourages applications from minorities and women. Learn More About PC About PC History, Mission, & Plan Benefits & Resources Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: 503 South Broad Street Clinton, South Carolina 29325 800.476.7272 Contact Us facebook instagram x flickr Quick Links & Resources Quick Links & Resources News Events Directory Athletics Campus Map Living on Campus Academic Calendar Course Catalog Offices & Facilities Accreditation Job Opportunities Justice & Community Wellbeing First-Gen Students International Students Accessible Education Health & Wellness Single-Sign On BannerWeb Brightspace Campus Bookstore Library Online Payment Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community Search for: © Copyright 2026 Presbyterian College | Title IX Policies Consumer Information Campus Police Presbyterian College is committed to providing an accessible and inclusive online experience. If you are unable to access any part of this website due to a disability, please contact Accessible Education at [email protected] for assistance. NOTICE OF NONDISCRIMINATORY POLICY AS TO STUDENTS: Presbyterian College is an equal educational opportunity institution. The College’s admission standards and practices are free from discrimination on the basis of age, sex, race, creed, color, disability, ethnicity or national origin. Apply Info Visit Give Search for: Resources For Close Menu Current Students Accepted Students Faculty & Staff Alumni & Friends First-Gen Students Community"
